{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The target claims the distinction between independent and correlated failures is 'uniquely evidenced' by temporal signature/Poisson distribution, but its own first countercondition refutes this uniqueness: a slow-burn shared cause (e.g., memory leak triggered by local load) produces staggered failures that do NOT cluster tightly around a singular t-zero event and can mimic a Poisson process. The problem asks for evidence that would distinguish failures that 'merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found'; a slow-burn shared cause is precisely such a case, and the target's temporal-signature mechanism cannot detect it because the variance in failure start-times will not be tight around any known external event. So the claimed unique evidentiary value of the temporal signature fails against the very countercondition the target admits.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"The target argues for decentralized resilience by attacking 'cascading synchronization' in large services' failover, but its own second countercondition concedes that if the large service's redundancy has sufficient headroom to absorb failover load, the cascade mechanism does not occur. Since the problem statement compares against a large service with 'strictly more total internal redundancy,' that extra redundancy can be precisely the headroom that absorbs the load shift. The target therefore does not establish that the decentralized network is more resilient under the problem's own comparison condition; it only addresses the subset of large services that lack such headroom.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_003","attack":true,"case":"The target's claim relies on heterogeneity as a resilience advantage, but its second countercondition concedes that if the failure trigger is universal to the protocol/standard (e.g., a logic error in the core network standard), heterogeneity in implementation does not protect. The problem asks to argue the decentralized network is more resilient to correlated failure, and protocol-level triggers are a canonical class of correlated failure that the problem explicitly wants distinguished ('failures that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found'). Because decentralized services communicating via a shared protocol can all share the same protocol trigger while appearing operationally diverse, the heterogeneity mechanism fails to establish superiority under a genuinely correlated failure mode the problem foregrounds.","counterexample":null}]}